PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B. I, II, III

Colleague 8’s emphasis on documented trade-offs in low-supervision or diversion-prone settings is the clearest way to reconcile the statements without forcing an artificial exclusion. Statement I is supported because the naloxone component is explicitly designed to raise the barrier to parenteral misuse through precipitated withdrawal; that mechanism creates a distinct risk layer precisely when injection occurs, and some prescribing standards and post-marketing surveillance still reference Suboxone as comparatively less safe in those specific contexts. This does not contradict II, which correctly flags the narrow, guideline-supported exceptions (pregnancy, documented naloxone sensitivity) where any exposure is avoided by choosing Subutex monotherapy. Statement III then completes the picture by confirming that, when both formulations are taken sublingually as directed, naloxone bioavailability is negligible and the therapeutic safety/efficacy profiles converge on buprenorphine alone. 

The repeated claim that I “inverts” the abuse-deterrent rationale overlooks how that same deterrent intent produces the documented perception of added risk in unsupervised settings; the three statements together therefore capture the full, evidence-based clinical nuance without introducing the factual reversals or unsupported uncertainty claims present in IV and V. Maintaining B preserves this balance while still aligning with the core pharmacology and guideline references already cited.}

