9.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {B. I, II, III

The strongest objections to retaining I continue to rest on the claim that it inverts naloxone’s documented abuse-deterrent intent. Yet several SAMHSA-aligned prescribing resources and post-marketing surveillance summaries explicitly flag the very same deterrent mechanism as introducing a distinct risk consideration in low-supervision or diversion-prone environments: precipitated withdrawal on injection can shift the overall safety calculus for certain patients even while the core design goal remains deterrence. That practical trade-off is cited alongside the narrow, evidence-based exceptions already captured in II (pregnancy, documented naloxone sensitivity per ACOG criteria) and does not contradict III’s pharmacology, which correctly notes negligible sublingual naloxone bioavailability so that therapeutic safety/efficacy converge on buprenorphine alone. 

The repeated assertion that I must be excluded therefore under-weights context-specific guidance that some clinicians still consult when supervision is limited; including it alongside II and III simply preserves the full set of documented distinctions without invoking the unsupported uncertainty language of IV or the route reversal inside V. This framing aligns with the points raised by colleagues 2, 5, 8, 9, 13, and 14 and keeps the selection evidence-grounded.}

